WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:09.950
On the moon where the sun's shining

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.353
it's blindingly bright.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.433
Where it isn't, it's pitch black.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:20.263
The surface is covered in
the finest particles of sand.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.170
It's a tricky environment

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.943
for even the most advanced
machines to navigate.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.740
And before fleets of
these things start roaming

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.033
the real moon, this engineer
has many late nights ahead.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.520
Perfecting this rover and testing it

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.040
on an artificial landscape like this one

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.860
will get us one step
closer to establishing

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.093
the first human settlement
away from Earth.

00:00:49.301 --> 00:00:51.970
Discovery, roger, go for deploy.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.160
One day when you and I
make it up to the moon,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.200
it could be thanks to her.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.200
My name is Akane Imamura

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.163
and I built miniature lunar rover.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.680
Going to the moon used to be something

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.923
only the biggest governments dreamed of.

00:01:47.875 --> 00:01:50.530
But with the growing
commercialization of space

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.470
some of the most exciting projects now

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.343
are taking place at startups.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.373
And today I'm back in my
hometown to visit one of them.

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:20.590
iSpace got its start in
2010 as a group of engineers

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.103
competing for the $30
million Google Lunar XPRIZE.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.040
The competition expired
last year without a winner.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.930
But having raised more than $90 million

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.700
from Japan's government-backed
funds and companies

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.480
including Japan Airlines,
iSpace is now focused

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.010
on becoming a business in its own right.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.010
So this is the rover?

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.847
Yeah, this is the rover.

00:03:03.681 --> 00:03:04.514
And I'll tell ya,

00:03:04.514 --> 00:03:06.364
Indy's never seen a driver like this.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.270
When you think of a space rover,

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.000
you might think of the moon buggy

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.520
used in three of the Apollo missions.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.353
There's a big crater.

00:03:15.353 --> 00:03:16.186
They're the ones.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.780
Or the Opportunity rover

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.683
that looked for water on Mars.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.530
Next to these two hulking
creations from previous decades,

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.933
iSpace's machine is so
small it looks like a toy.

00:03:40.517 --> 00:03:41.350
Flip it over?

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.190
Yeah.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.780
Akane's team is racing
against their deadline

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.780
of 2021 when a SpaceX rocket
will blast iSpace's lander

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.883
carrying two rovers into
orbit around the moon.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.600
After a soft landing,
each rover will roll out

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.850
and roam the lunar landscape,
sending images, video,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.813
and mapping data back to Earth.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.490
Tomorrow Akane's team

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.390
will take one big step forward.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.370
They're going to be taking

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.913
these two prototypes to a fake moon.

00:04:43.253 --> 00:04:44.090
Akane's responsible

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.363
for the structural elements of the Rover.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.390
And that involves everything from choosing

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.280
the best physical materials
to make a lightweight

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.630
but still resilient machine
to the thorny challenge

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.723
of designing the shape of these wheels.

00:05:32.504 --> 00:05:34.440
Early the next morning we made our way

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.503
to the suburbs outside Tokyo.

00:05:56.734 --> 00:05:58.200
And once the city's high-rises

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.510
give way to smaller neighborhoods

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.810
we reached our destination,

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.143
the Japan Aerospace Exploration
Agency, known as JAXA.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.643
Here on Earth this is the
closest thing to the moon.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.550
We have an hour, hour and
a half of real testing

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.900
this morning with both Rover A and Rover B

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.780
doing their tests in parallel.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.633
Make sure that we're logging
and backing up all the data.

00:06:28.633 --> 00:06:33.633
Okay, let's do it.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.073
And after a few final preparations
they started the tests.

00:07:37.493 --> 00:07:38.800
At first was a series

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.593
of 10 meter crawls along flat ground.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.390
With every run, Akane's
team reconfigured the rover

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.390
to have a different weight distribution.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:53.910
The plan was to see which versions

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.410
would be able to run straight.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.780
And measure how much

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.530
the rogue ones would veer off-path.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:10.230
And then it was the
hardest part of the day,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.083
the lunar hill climbs.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.970
And with a chance to play a very tiny role

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:20.940
in establishing humanity's presence

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.393
on the moon, I couldn't resist.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.600
Dear future lunar residents

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.703
and lunar tourists, you're welcome.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:36.165
Bye bye.

00:09:36.165 --> 00:09:36.998
Bye.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.943
For about two days a week,
Akane works from home.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.810
And it's this flexibility
that's helped her

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:07.167
balance a job as daunting
as building a space rover--

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:11.610
With another equally
demanding responsibility.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.350
But in a country that has one

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.640
of the developed world's worst
records on gender inequality,

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.890
Akane's success is still rare,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.493
and even for her a pretty
recent development.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.310
Akane has a Master's in
Mechanical Engineering

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.000
and her first job out of
school involved research

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.230
on concrete and later on metals

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.680
at a big prestigious
Japanese manufacturer.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.700
She was one of very few female engineers

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.503
in an R&amp;D department dominated by men.

00:11:26.781 --> 00:11:28.490
Several times Akane considered

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:32.200
quitting work altogether and
becoming a full-time housewife

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.413
while her husband pursued his career.

00:11:55.116 --> 00:11:57.490
In 2017, Akane joined iSpace

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.833
as a spacecraft structure engineer.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:05.040
The hectic nature of life at a startup

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.600
has come with its own challenges.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.040
But in a company that's
attracted young people

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.070
from countries like
France, the US, and Canada

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.823
she says she's a lot happier now.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.950
iSpace predicts its city on the moon

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.683
will be ready in 2040.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.860
By then, with an estimated $1 trillion

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.350
global space industry to serve
we'll have a whole generation

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:33.080
of engineers building machines

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.943
to explore the moon, Mars, and beyond.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:41.180
Now that they've successfully
completed their field tests,

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.300
Akane and her coworkers have been busy

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.870
analyzing all the data they collected

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:48.993
so they can start designing the
next iteration of the rover.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.230
Their first true milestone

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.053
will be the rover's launch
day in two years' time.

